<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  bluerobotics_parts — the parts-level macro library.

  PROTOTYPE. One part is wired up (blueboat_antenna_mast); the rest of the
  catalog follows the same one line once each part has a macro.

  Include this once and every part is available as a macro. An assembly should
  not include individual part files directly — that is what this file is for.

      <xacro:include filename="$(find bluerobotics_parts)/urdf/parts.xacro"/>
      <xacro:blueboat_antenna_mast name="mast" parent="base_link" xyz="0 0 0.2"/>

  ===== the part macro contract =====

  Every macro included below takes exactly these parameters, in this order:

      name       link name for this instance; the fixed joint is <name>_joint
      parent     link to attach to
      xyz / rpy  mount pose relative to parent (meters / radians)
      collision  false to instantiate the part with no contact geometry

  This is a contract, not a convention. A dispatcher that mounts parts from a
  config list forwards exactly this set to whichever macro a `type` names, so a
  macro that drops or renames one fails with `Invalid parameter "collision"`
  instead of doing something surprising. The vessel macros will have to honor
  it too.

  That dispatcher (`mount_parts`, generalized from accessories.xacro) is not
  here yet. It belongs with the assembly work, where it can be exercised
  against a real vehicle rather than against itself.

  Mass, inertia tensor and center-of-gravity pose are carried by each part in
  its own file, hand-authored from measurement or the vendor. There is no
  central mass table here and so nothing to keep in sync. See
  AUR_BUOYANCY_DESIGN.md.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <!-- ===== catalog =====
       One include per part. Keep alphabetical; the directory name, the file
       name and the macro name are all the part name from models/parts.csv. -->
  <xacro:include filename="$(find bluerobotics_parts)/models/blueboat_antenna_mast/blueboat_antenna_mast.urdf.xacro"/>

</robot>
